1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,669
Some of the most advanced fighter jets

2
00:00:02,669 --> 00:00:04,847
in the world are designed to be

3
00:00:04,847 --> 00:00:05,479
unstable.

4
00:00:05,659 --> 00:00:08,900
Without a computer correcting them many times

5
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:11,492
every second, they would flip out of

6
00:00:11,492 --> 00:00:12,068
control.

7
00:00:12,248 --> 00:00:14,276
Their tails lean sideways,

8
00:00:14,276 --> 00:00:16,070
their doors have teeth,

9
00:00:16,070 --> 00:00:19,113
their pilots lie almost on their backs,

10
00:00:19,113 --> 00:00:21,531
and some of them have a strange

11
00:00:21,531 --> 00:00:24,183
bump right in front of the engine.

12
00:00:24,363 --> 00:00:25,663
It all looks wrong.

13
00:00:25,843 --> 00:00:28,714
Today, ten fighter jet parts that look

14
00:00:28,714 --> 00:00:31,131
wrong, and why every one of them

15
00:00:31,131 --> 00:00:31,811
is right.

16
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:34,966
A fighter jet is a compromise between

17
00:00:34,966 --> 00:00:37,164
things that fight each other.

18
00:00:37,344 --> 00:00:38,988
It has to turn hard,

19
00:00:38,988 --> 00:00:40,796
fly faster than sound,

20
00:00:40,796 --> 00:00:42,110
hide from radar,

21
00:00:42,110 --> 00:00:45,315
carry weapons, and bring the pilot home

22
00:00:45,315 --> 00:00:45,808
alive.

23
00:00:45,988 --> 00:00:48,817
An airliner is designed to be calm

24
00:00:48,817 --> 00:00:49,982
and efficient.

25
00:00:50,162 --> 00:00:52,693
A fighter is designed to win.

26
00:00:52,873 --> 00:00:55,080
And that is why its shape breaks

27
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:57,769
almost every rule you would expect from

28
00:00:57,769 --> 00:00:58,597
an airplane.

29
00:00:58,946 --> 00:01:01,947
As always, every fact here comes from

30
00:01:01,947 --> 00:01:03,164
a named source,

31
00:01:03,164 --> 00:01:05,921
the manufacturers, the air forces,

32
00:01:05,921 --> 00:01:07,868
and aviation references,

33
00:01:07,868 --> 00:01:10,301
all listed in the description.

34
00:01:10,481 --> 00:01:12,964
Let's start with the little wings in

35
00:01:12,964 --> 00:01:14,550
front of the big wings,

36
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:16,480
and the idea of a plane that

37
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:18,480
wants to fall out of the sky.

38
00:01:18,830 --> 00:01:21,970
Why do jets like the Eurofighter Typhoon,

39
00:01:21,970 --> 00:01:24,727
the Rafale and the Gripen have small

40
00:01:24,727 --> 00:01:27,178
wings in front of the main wing?

41
00:01:27,358 --> 00:01:28,965
They are called canards,

42
00:01:28,965 --> 00:01:31,443
and they are there because these jets

43
00:01:31,443 --> 00:01:33,117
are built to be unstable.

44
00:01:33,297 --> 00:01:35,964
A stable airplane wants to keep flying

45
00:01:35,964 --> 00:01:38,491
straight, and it fights you when you

46
00:01:38,491 --> 00:01:39,334
try to turn.

47
00:01:39,514 --> 00:01:43,225
An unstable airplane wants to change direction

48
00:01:43,225 --> 00:01:44,274
all the time.

49
00:01:44,454 --> 00:01:46,370
That makes it much more agile.

50
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:49,711
The canards help control that instability and

51
00:01:49,711 --> 00:01:52,099
push the nose around very quickly.

52
00:01:52,449 --> 00:01:55,053
The catch is that no human can

53
00:01:55,053 --> 00:01:57,918
fly an unstable airplane by hand.

54
00:01:58,098 --> 00:02:00,756
So these jets use fly by wire.

55
00:02:00,936 --> 00:02:03,408
The pilot's stick does not move the

56
00:02:03,408 --> 00:02:05,244
control surfaces directly.

57
00:02:05,424 --> 00:02:08,146
It sends a request to flight computers,

58
00:02:08,146 --> 00:02:10,799
and the computers move the canards and

59
00:02:10,799 --> 00:02:13,731
the other surfaces many times every second

60
00:02:13,731 --> 00:02:16,175
to keep the airplane under control.

61
00:02:16,355 --> 00:02:18,614
If all the computers failed,

62
00:02:18,614 --> 00:02:21,196
the airplane could not be flown.

63
00:02:21,376 --> 00:02:23,125
That is why there are several of

64
00:02:23,125 --> 00:02:24,545
them, checking each other.

65
00:02:24,895 --> 00:02:28,378
Canards also help on takeoff and landing.

66
00:02:28,558 --> 00:02:30,951
Because they lift the nose from the

67
00:02:30,951 --> 00:02:33,617
front, the airplane can rotate at lower

68
00:02:33,617 --> 00:02:35,257
speeds and land shorter.

69
00:02:35,437 --> 00:02:37,748
On a delta wing jet without a

70
00:02:37,748 --> 00:02:40,615
tail, they also act as the elevator.

71
00:02:40,795 --> 00:02:43,454
Small wings, big job.

72
00:02:43,634 --> 00:02:45,458
And they are the first clue that

73
00:02:45,458 --> 00:02:47,338
a fighter jet is a different kind

74
00:02:47,338 --> 00:02:47,965
of machine.

75
00:02:48,314 --> 00:02:50,573
There is a reason this matters so

76
00:02:50,573 --> 00:02:50,915
much.

77
00:02:51,095 --> 00:02:54,130
Older fighters were designed to be stable,

78
00:02:54,130 --> 00:02:56,732
like airliners, and they paid for it

79
00:02:56,732 --> 00:02:57,527
in agility.

80
00:02:57,707 --> 00:03:00,424
Fly by wire changed the rules.

81
00:03:00,604 --> 00:03:03,656
The F sixteen was the first production

82
00:03:03,656 --> 00:03:08,073
fighter intentionally designed to be slightly unstable,

83
00:03:08,073 --> 00:03:11,446
and almost every modern fighter since then

84
00:03:11,446 --> 00:03:13,293
has followed that path.

85
00:03:13,473 --> 00:03:16,082
The canards are simply the most visible

86
00:03:16,082 --> 00:03:16,817
sign of it.

87
00:03:17,167 --> 00:03:19,317
Why do the wings of jets like

88
00:03:19,317 --> 00:03:22,579
the F eighteen Hornet stretch forward almost

89
00:03:22,579 --> 00:03:23,691
to the cockpit,

90
00:03:23,691 --> 00:03:25,248
in a thin sharp edge?

91
00:03:25,428 --> 00:03:28,249
It is called a leading edge extension.

92
00:03:28,429 --> 00:03:29,602
At high angles,

93
00:03:29,602 --> 00:03:32,260
when the nose points far above the

94
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:33,823
direction of flight,

95
00:03:33,823 --> 00:03:35,465
a normal wing stalls.

96
00:03:35,645 --> 00:03:39,525
The sharp extension creates a powerful spinning

97
00:03:39,525 --> 00:03:42,250
vortex of air that flows over the

98
00:03:42,250 --> 00:03:45,057
wing and keeps the air attached to

99
00:03:45,057 --> 00:03:45,304
it.

100
00:03:45,484 --> 00:03:47,931
That lets the jet keep flying and

101
00:03:47,931 --> 00:03:50,971
turning at angles that would make another

102
00:03:50,971 --> 00:03:52,009
airplane fall.

103
00:03:52,359 --> 00:03:54,786
You can actually see these vortices.

104
00:03:54,966 --> 00:03:56,163
On a humid day,

105
00:03:56,163 --> 00:03:57,361
at an air show,

106
00:03:57,361 --> 00:03:59,595
when a Hornet pulls up hard,

107
00:03:59,595 --> 00:04:03,267
white spirals of condensation appear along the

108
00:04:03,267 --> 00:04:04,624
top of the wings,

109
00:04:04,624 --> 00:04:07,098
starting from those extensions.

110
00:04:07,278 --> 00:04:09,301
That is the air being spun into

111
00:04:09,301 --> 00:04:11,388
a tornado that holds the jet up.

112
00:04:11,568 --> 00:04:13,800
It is one of the reasons the

113
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:17,068
Hornet can point its nose almost anywhere

114
00:04:17,068 --> 00:04:18,105
at low speed.

115
00:04:18,455 --> 00:04:21,035
The same idea shows up on many

116
00:04:21,035 --> 00:04:24,992
modern fighters, sometimes as small strakes on

117
00:04:24,992 --> 00:04:26,454
the nose or body.

118
00:04:26,634 --> 00:04:28,933
The Su twenty-seven family,

119
00:04:28,933 --> 00:04:31,659
the F sixteen and the F eighteen

120
00:04:31,659 --> 00:04:34,981
all use sharp extensions where the wing

121
00:04:34,981 --> 00:04:36,258
meets the body.

122
00:04:36,438 --> 00:04:40,365
Engineers learned that a carefully shaped vortex

123
00:04:40,365 --> 00:04:42,655
is like a free extra wing at

124
00:04:42,655 --> 00:04:43,637
high angles.

125
00:04:43,817 --> 00:04:46,745
It weighs almost nothing and adds lift

126
00:04:46,745 --> 00:04:49,443
exactly when the jet needs it most,

127
00:04:49,443 --> 00:04:50,291
in a tight,

128
00:04:50,291 --> 00:04:51,061
slow turn.

129
00:04:51,412 --> 00:04:53,664
Why do the twin tails on jets

130
00:04:53,664 --> 00:04:56,072
like the F twenty-two and the F

131
00:04:56,072 --> 00:05:00,188
thirty-five lean outward instead of standing straight

132
00:05:00,188 --> 00:05:00,421
up?

133
00:05:00,601 --> 00:05:03,294
One big reason is radar.

134
00:05:03,474 --> 00:05:06,330
A vertical tail meeting a flat wing

135
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:09,349
or body makes a ninety degree corner.

136
00:05:09,529 --> 00:05:12,462
Corners like that bounce radar waves straight

137
00:05:12,462 --> 00:05:14,352
back to where they came from,

138
00:05:14,352 --> 00:05:15,264
like a mirror.

139
00:05:15,444 --> 00:05:18,593
Tilting the tails breaks that corner,

140
00:05:18,593 --> 00:05:21,742
so the radar energy is reflected away

141
00:05:21,742 --> 00:05:24,381
into the sky instead of back to

142
00:05:24,381 --> 00:05:25,232
the enemy.

143
00:05:25,582 --> 00:05:28,874
Two tails also help at high angles

144
00:05:28,874 --> 00:05:29,842
of attack.

145
00:05:30,022 --> 00:05:32,649
When the nose is pointed very high,

146
00:05:32,649 --> 00:05:34,976
a single tail in the middle can

147
00:05:34,976 --> 00:05:36,927
be hidden behind the body,

148
00:05:36,927 --> 00:05:38,203
in turbulent air,

149
00:05:38,203 --> 00:05:39,554
and lose its grip.

150
00:05:39,734 --> 00:05:42,584
Two tails placed farther apart stay in

151
00:05:42,584 --> 00:05:43,484
cleaner air.

152
00:05:43,664 --> 00:05:45,843
So the same feature makes the jet

153
00:05:45,843 --> 00:05:48,221
harder to see and easier to control,

154
00:05:48,221 --> 00:05:50,862
two goals that usually fight each other.

155
00:05:51,213 --> 00:05:54,592
Stealth designers think of every surface as

156
00:05:54,592 --> 00:05:55,299
a mirror.

157
00:05:55,479 --> 00:05:57,488
The trick is not to make the

158
00:05:57,488 --> 00:06:00,430
airplane invisible, but to make sure that

159
00:06:00,430 --> 00:06:02,870
the radar energy that hits it goes

160
00:06:02,870 --> 00:06:05,812
almost anywhere except back to the radar.

161
00:06:05,992 --> 00:06:07,094
So the edges,

162
00:06:07,094 --> 00:06:10,144
angles and surfaces of a stealth jet

163
00:06:10,144 --> 00:06:12,516
are aligned with each other,

164
00:06:12,516 --> 00:06:14,804
in a few chosen directions.

165
00:06:14,984 --> 00:06:18,055
That idea explains the next strange part

166
00:06:18,055 --> 00:06:18,363
too.

167
00:06:18,713 --> 00:06:21,170
Why do the doors and panels on

168
00:06:21,170 --> 00:06:23,709
stealth jets have zigzag edges,

169
00:06:23,709 --> 00:06:24,610
like teeth?

170
00:06:24,790 --> 00:06:28,233
Because every edge on an airplane reflects

171
00:06:28,233 --> 00:06:31,512
radar, especially an edge that faces the

172
00:06:31,512 --> 00:06:32,987
radar straight on.

173
00:06:33,167 --> 00:06:35,918
A straight door edge would create a

174
00:06:35,918 --> 00:06:36,940
bright flash.

175
00:06:37,120 --> 00:06:39,971
The zigzag edges are angled to match

176
00:06:39,971 --> 00:06:42,425
the main edges of the wings and

177
00:06:42,425 --> 00:06:42,821
tail.

178
00:06:43,001 --> 00:06:45,507
That way, all the edges send their

179
00:06:45,507 --> 00:06:48,381
reflections in the same few directions,

180
00:06:48,381 --> 00:06:51,476
instead of lighting up in every direction.

181
00:06:51,826 --> 00:06:54,403
You can see the teeth on landing

182
00:06:54,403 --> 00:06:56,738
gear doors, weapon bay doors,

183
00:06:56,738 --> 00:06:58,188
and access panels.

184
00:06:58,368 --> 00:07:01,189
Even small gaps and screws matter.

185
00:07:01,369 --> 00:07:02,957
On stealth aircraft,

186
00:07:02,957 --> 00:07:06,529
maintenance crews treat panel edges and seams

187
00:07:06,529 --> 00:07:09,783
carefully, because a badly sealed gap can

188
00:07:09,783 --> 00:07:12,561
make the jet more visible to radar.

189
00:07:12,741 --> 00:07:15,001
A fighter can be stealthy in the

190
00:07:15,001 --> 00:07:18,604
factory and noticeably less stealthy after careless

191
00:07:18,604 --> 00:07:19,452
maintenance.

192
00:07:19,802 --> 00:07:23,293
Stealth also changes how a jet carries

193
00:07:23,293 --> 00:07:24,028
weapons.

194
00:07:24,208 --> 00:07:27,507
Missiles and bombs hanging under the wings

195
00:07:27,507 --> 00:07:29,864
are full of edges and corners,

196
00:07:29,864 --> 00:07:32,535
so stealth jets carry them inside,

197
00:07:32,535 --> 00:07:35,520
in weapon bays with those same toothed

198
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:35,992
doors.

199
00:07:36,172 --> 00:07:37,750
The doors snap open,

200
00:07:37,750 --> 00:07:39,406
the weapon drops out,

201
00:07:39,406 --> 00:07:41,773
and the doors close again in a

202
00:07:41,773 --> 00:07:42,325
moment.

203
00:07:42,505 --> 00:07:43,990
For that short time,

204
00:07:43,990 --> 00:07:46,144
the jet is a little easier to

205
00:07:46,144 --> 00:07:46,441
see.

206
00:07:46,621 --> 00:07:48,327
Then it disappears again.

207
00:07:48,678 --> 00:07:50,694
Why do some fighter jets,

208
00:07:50,694 --> 00:07:52,953
like the F sixteen and the F

209
00:07:52,953 --> 00:07:56,099
twenty-two, have a canopy with a golden

210
00:07:56,099 --> 00:07:56,503
tint?

211
00:07:56,683 --> 00:07:58,907
Because the cockpit is one of the

212
00:07:58,907 --> 00:08:01,536
brightest things on a fighter on radar.

213
00:08:01,716 --> 00:08:05,029
Radar waves go straight through clear glass

214
00:08:05,029 --> 00:08:07,880
and bounce around inside the cockpit,

215
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:09,036
off the helmet,

216
00:08:09,036 --> 00:08:11,271
the seat and the instruments.

217
00:08:11,451 --> 00:08:14,400
A very thin metallic coating on the

218
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:17,770
canopy, often based on indium tin oxide,

219
00:08:17,770 --> 00:08:21,393
reflects radar away while still letting the

220
00:08:21,393 --> 00:08:22,573
pilot see out.

221
00:08:22,923 --> 00:08:25,997
The coating also blocks some heat and

222
00:08:25,997 --> 00:08:27,576
light from the sun,

223
00:08:27,576 --> 00:08:30,401
which helps the pilot in a cockpit

224
00:08:30,401 --> 00:08:32,977
that sits under a big bubble of

225
00:08:32,977 --> 00:08:33,476
glass.

226
00:08:33,656 --> 00:08:36,233
And the bubble itself is a design

227
00:08:36,233 --> 00:08:36,779
choice.

228
00:08:36,959 --> 00:08:40,756
The F sixteen's frameless bubble canopy gives

229
00:08:40,756 --> 00:08:43,371
the pilot a view almost all the

230
00:08:43,371 --> 00:08:45,818
way around, including behind.

231
00:08:45,998 --> 00:08:46,980
In a dogfight,

232
00:08:46,980 --> 00:08:49,646
seeing the enemy first can matter more

233
00:08:49,646 --> 00:08:50,769
than any weapon.

234
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:53,706
The pilot's helmet is part of the

235
00:08:53,706 --> 00:08:54,568
system too.

236
00:08:54,748 --> 00:08:56,382
On the F thirty-five,

237
00:08:56,382 --> 00:08:59,652
the helmet displays flight data and images

238
00:08:59,652 --> 00:09:01,831
from cameras around the jet,

239
00:09:01,831 --> 00:09:04,166
so the pilot can look down and

240
00:09:04,166 --> 00:09:07,124
see through the floor of the airplane.

241
00:09:07,304 --> 00:09:10,104
The canopy, the helmet and the sensors

242
00:09:10,104 --> 00:09:11,135
work together.

243
00:09:11,315 --> 00:09:13,065
The cockpit is not just a seat

244
00:09:13,065 --> 00:09:13,765
with a view.

245
00:09:13,945 --> 00:09:17,160
It is a sensor station built around

246
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:18,171
one person.

247
00:09:18,521 --> 00:09:21,018
Why do jets like the F thirty-five

248
00:09:21,018 --> 00:09:23,515
have a strange bump right in front

249
00:09:23,515 --> 00:09:25,057
of the engine intake?

250
00:09:25,237 --> 00:09:28,987
It is called a diverterless supersonic inlet.

251
00:09:29,167 --> 00:09:31,661
Air flowing along the body of the

252
00:09:31,661 --> 00:09:32,946
jet forms a slow,

253
00:09:32,946 --> 00:09:35,213
messy layer close to the skin,

254
00:09:35,213 --> 00:09:37,178
and engines hate that air.

255
00:09:37,358 --> 00:09:39,368
Older jets used a gap and a

256
00:09:39,368 --> 00:09:41,677
separate plate to push it away.

257
00:09:41,857 --> 00:09:44,208
The bump does the same job with

258
00:09:44,208 --> 00:09:45,421
no moving parts.

259
00:09:45,601 --> 00:09:48,164
Its shape pushes the slow air to

260
00:09:48,164 --> 00:09:50,966
the sides, so only clean air enters

261
00:09:50,966 --> 00:09:51,847
the engine.

262
00:09:52,198 --> 00:09:55,276
Lockheed Martin developed it in the early

263
00:09:55,276 --> 00:09:58,130
nineteen nineties and first flew it on

264
00:09:58,130 --> 00:10:01,058
December eleventh, nineteen ninety-six,

265
00:10:01,058 --> 00:10:02,861
on a modified F sixteen,

266
00:10:02,861 --> 00:10:05,038
which still reached Mach two.

267
00:10:05,218 --> 00:10:07,666
In the Joint Strike Fighter studies,

268
00:10:07,666 --> 00:10:10,385
the bump design was about thirty percent

269
00:10:10,385 --> 00:10:12,764
lighter than the traditional inlet,

270
00:10:12,764 --> 00:10:15,076
and it removed the gaps and plates

271
00:10:15,076 --> 00:10:16,503
that reflected radar.

272
00:10:16,683 --> 00:10:19,412
Today it appears on several new fighters

273
00:10:19,412 --> 00:10:20,572
around the world.

274
00:10:20,923 --> 00:10:23,535
The bump also helps hide the engine.

275
00:10:23,715 --> 00:10:25,550
The front of a jet engine,

276
00:10:25,550 --> 00:10:27,103
with its spinning fan,

277
00:10:27,103 --> 00:10:29,715
is one of the brightest radar targets

278
00:10:29,715 --> 00:10:30,774
on an airplane.

279
00:10:30,954 --> 00:10:33,625
Curved intake ducts and shapes like the

280
00:10:33,625 --> 00:10:35,612
bump help keep the fan out of

281
00:10:35,612 --> 00:10:36,434
direct view.

282
00:10:36,614 --> 00:10:38,425
Stealth is not one trick.

283
00:10:38,605 --> 00:10:40,744
It is hundreds of small decisions,

284
00:10:40,744 --> 00:10:42,443
and this is one of the most

285
00:10:42,443 --> 00:10:42,947
visible.

286
00:10:43,297 --> 00:10:45,659
Why can the exhaust nozzles of the

287
00:10:45,659 --> 00:10:47,743
F twenty-two move up and down?

288
00:10:47,923 --> 00:10:50,234
It is called thrust vectoring.

289
00:10:50,414 --> 00:10:53,602
Normally an airplane turns by pushing air

290
00:10:53,602 --> 00:10:55,625
with its control surfaces,

291
00:10:55,625 --> 00:10:57,880
and at low speed there is not

292
00:10:57,880 --> 00:10:59,202
much air to push.

293
00:10:59,382 --> 00:11:02,635
The F twenty-two's engines can point their

294
00:11:02,635 --> 00:11:05,346
thrust as much as twenty degrees up

295
00:11:05,346 --> 00:11:05,965
or down.

296
00:11:06,145 --> 00:11:08,195
So even at very low speed,

297
00:11:08,195 --> 00:11:10,403
the jet can use the power of

298
00:11:10,403 --> 00:11:13,320
its engines to swing its nose around.

299
00:11:13,670 --> 00:11:15,432
The nozzles are flat,

300
00:11:15,432 --> 00:11:18,622
not round, which also helps reduce the

301
00:11:18,622 --> 00:11:21,727
heat and radar signature from behind.

302
00:11:21,907 --> 00:11:25,030
The movement is managed automatically by the

303
00:11:25,030 --> 00:11:28,792
flight computers, together with the control surfaces.

304
00:11:28,972 --> 00:11:31,688
The pilot does not steer the nozzles.

305
00:11:31,868 --> 00:11:34,219
He or she just pulls the stick,

306
00:11:34,219 --> 00:11:36,949
and the computers decide how much to

307
00:11:36,949 --> 00:11:39,072
use the tail and how much to

308
00:11:39,072 --> 00:11:40,286
use the engines.

309
00:11:40,636 --> 00:11:44,262
Russian fighters like the Sukhoi Su thirty-five

310
00:11:44,262 --> 00:11:46,885
use round nozzles that can move in

311
00:11:46,885 --> 00:11:49,971
more directions, and they are famous for

312
00:11:49,971 --> 00:11:53,674
slow, impossible looking maneuvers at air shows.

313
00:11:53,854 --> 00:11:56,856
Whether those moves matter in real combat

314
00:11:56,856 --> 00:11:57,662
is debated.

315
00:11:57,842 --> 00:11:59,102
But at low speed,

316
00:11:59,102 --> 00:12:01,770
with the nose pointed where a normal

317
00:12:01,770 --> 00:12:03,252
jet cannot point it,

318
00:12:03,252 --> 00:12:06,291
thrust vectoring gives the pilot one more

319
00:12:06,291 --> 00:12:07,106
way to aim.

320
00:12:07,457 --> 00:12:10,079
Why does the F sixteen pilot sit

321
00:12:10,079 --> 00:12:11,717
leaning so far back?

322
00:12:11,897 --> 00:12:15,166
The seat is reclined at thirty degrees,

323
00:12:15,166 --> 00:12:17,680
about twice as much as in most

324
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:18,434
fighters.

325
00:12:18,614 --> 00:12:20,913
The reason is g force.

326
00:12:21,093 --> 00:12:22,136
In a hard turn,

327
00:12:22,136 --> 00:12:24,639
blood is pulled from the head toward

328
00:12:24,639 --> 00:12:26,933
the feet, and the pilot can black

329
00:12:26,933 --> 00:12:27,211
out.

330
00:12:27,391 --> 00:12:30,411
Leaning back reduces the height between the

331
00:12:30,411 --> 00:12:31,745
heart and the eyes,

332
00:12:31,745 --> 00:12:33,852
so the heart has an easier job

333
00:12:33,852 --> 00:12:35,959
pushing blood up to the brain.

334
00:12:36,139 --> 00:12:39,298
It increases the pilot's tolerance to high

335
00:12:39,298 --> 00:12:39,448
g.

336
00:12:39,798 --> 00:12:42,474
The same jet moved the control stick

337
00:12:42,474 --> 00:12:44,928
from the middle of the cockpit to

338
00:12:44,928 --> 00:12:46,043
the right side,

339
00:12:46,043 --> 00:12:47,158
on the armrest.

340
00:12:47,338 --> 00:12:49,872
A side stick lets the pilot rest

341
00:12:49,872 --> 00:12:52,168
the arm while pulling high g,

342
00:12:52,168 --> 00:12:55,256
and make precise inputs with the wrist.

343
00:12:55,436 --> 00:12:58,285
Because the F sixteen uses fly by

344
00:12:58,285 --> 00:13:00,789
wire, the stick barely moves.

345
00:13:00,969 --> 00:13:03,751
It mostly senses how hard the pilot

346
00:13:03,751 --> 00:13:04,626
is pushing.

347
00:13:04,806 --> 00:13:05,537
In a long,

348
00:13:05,537 --> 00:13:08,317
hard turn, those small details are the

349
00:13:08,317 --> 00:13:11,902
difference between staying in control and getting

350
00:13:11,902 --> 00:13:12,341
tired.

351
00:13:12,691 --> 00:13:15,143
Even with the seat and the stick,

352
00:13:15,143 --> 00:13:17,743
pilots fight g force with their own

353
00:13:17,743 --> 00:13:18,263
bodies.

354
00:13:18,443 --> 00:13:19,923
They wear a g suit,

355
00:13:19,923 --> 00:13:24,050
trousers with air bladders that inflate automatically

356
00:13:24,050 --> 00:13:26,464
and squeeze the legs and belly,

357
00:13:26,464 --> 00:13:29,034
to keep blood from pooling in the

358
00:13:29,034 --> 00:13:29,891
lower body.

359
00:13:30,071 --> 00:13:32,666
And they use a breathing and straining

360
00:13:32,666 --> 00:13:35,672
technique, tensing the muscles to keep blood

361
00:13:35,672 --> 00:13:36,491
in the head.

362
00:13:36,671 --> 00:13:39,458
Pulling nine g is hard physical work.

363
00:13:39,808 --> 00:13:41,977
Why is there a yellow and black

364
00:13:41,977 --> 00:13:44,636
striped handle in front of the pilot's

365
00:13:44,636 --> 00:13:44,986
seat?

366
00:13:45,166 --> 00:13:47,000
That is the ejection handle.

367
00:13:47,180 --> 00:13:49,270
Pull it, and in less than a

368
00:13:49,270 --> 00:13:51,903
second the canopy is blown away or

369
00:13:51,903 --> 00:13:54,845
shattered, a rocket fires the seat out

370
00:13:54,845 --> 00:13:55,697
of the jet,

371
00:13:55,697 --> 00:13:57,090
a parachute opens,

372
00:13:57,090 --> 00:13:59,568
and the seat falls away from the

373
00:13:59,568 --> 00:14:00,032
pilot.

374
00:14:00,212 --> 00:14:02,581
The yellow and black stripes are the

375
00:14:02,581 --> 00:14:03,765
universal warning.

376
00:14:03,945 --> 00:14:05,117
On the ground,

377
00:14:05,117 --> 00:14:07,712
the seat is made safe with pins

378
00:14:07,712 --> 00:14:10,642
and red remove before flight flags,

379
00:14:10,642 --> 00:14:13,488
so nobody can fire it by accident.

380
00:14:13,839 --> 00:14:16,939
Modern seats are zero zero seats.

381
00:14:17,119 --> 00:14:19,941
That means they can save the pilot

382
00:14:19,941 --> 00:14:23,014
even at zero altitude and zero speed,

383
00:14:23,014 --> 00:14:25,338
sitting still on the runway.

384
00:14:25,518 --> 00:14:27,732
The seat is fired high enough for

385
00:14:27,732 --> 00:14:30,482
the parachute to open before reaching the

386
00:14:30,482 --> 00:14:30,952
ground.

387
00:14:31,132 --> 00:14:33,122
According to Martin-Baker,

388
00:14:33,122 --> 00:14:36,031
the British company that makes many of

389
00:14:36,031 --> 00:14:39,092
them, its seats had saved seven thousand

390
00:14:39,092 --> 00:14:42,537
eight hundred twenty-seven lives as of August

391
00:14:42,537 --> 00:14:43,914
twenty twenty-six.

392
00:14:44,265 --> 00:14:46,203
Ejecting is violent.

393
00:14:46,383 --> 00:14:49,384
The acceleration can injure the spine,

394
00:14:49,384 --> 00:14:52,384
which is one reason pilots are trained

395
00:14:52,384 --> 00:14:55,147
to sit in a precise position before

396
00:14:55,147 --> 00:14:56,647
pulling the handle.

397
00:14:56,827 --> 00:14:59,300
But compared to staying in a doomed

398
00:14:59,300 --> 00:15:01,561
jet, it is the best option there

399
00:15:01,561 --> 00:15:01,773
is.

400
00:15:01,953 --> 00:15:04,375
Every one of those thousands of saved

401
00:15:04,375 --> 00:15:07,125
lives began with someone pulling a striped

402
00:15:07,125 --> 00:15:07,583
handle.

403
00:15:07,934 --> 00:15:10,086
Why do navy fighters have a big

404
00:15:10,086 --> 00:15:11,475
hook under the tail?

405
00:15:11,655 --> 00:15:14,555
Because an aircraft carrier deck is far

406
00:15:14,555 --> 00:15:16,786
too short to land on normally.

407
00:15:16,966 --> 00:15:18,871
As the jet touches down,

408
00:15:18,871 --> 00:15:21,809
the hook catches one of several steel

409
00:15:21,809 --> 00:15:24,428
cables stretched across the deck,

410
00:15:24,428 --> 00:15:26,254
called arresting wires.

411
00:15:26,434 --> 00:15:29,831
The wires are connected to hydraulic engines

412
00:15:29,831 --> 00:15:32,688
below the deck that absorb the energy

413
00:15:32,688 --> 00:15:35,081
and stop a jet going around one

414
00:15:35,081 --> 00:15:37,861
hundred fifty miles an hour in about

415
00:15:37,861 --> 00:15:38,787
two seconds.

416
00:15:39,138 --> 00:15:41,262
Here is the part that looks crazy.

417
00:15:41,442 --> 00:15:43,832
When a navy jet touches down on

418
00:15:43,832 --> 00:15:46,993
a carrier, the pilot pushes the throttles

419
00:15:46,993 --> 00:15:49,846
to full power instead of pulling them

420
00:15:49,846 --> 00:15:50,231
back.

421
00:15:50,411 --> 00:15:52,943
If the hook misses all the wires,

422
00:15:52,943 --> 00:15:54,862
which is called a bolter,

423
00:15:54,862 --> 00:15:57,394
the jet needs enough power to fly

424
00:15:57,394 --> 00:15:59,466
off the end of the deck and

425
00:15:59,466 --> 00:16:00,233
try again.

426
00:16:00,413 --> 00:16:02,113
If the hook catches,

427
00:16:02,113 --> 00:16:04,662
the wire stops the jet anyway.

428
00:16:04,842 --> 00:16:07,562
Full power on landing is the safe

429
00:16:07,562 --> 00:16:08,140
choice.

430
00:16:08,490 --> 00:16:11,860
Navy jets also have stronger landing gear,

431
00:16:11,860 --> 00:16:14,588
because carrier landings are hard,

432
00:16:14,588 --> 00:16:17,958
controlled crashes onto the deck without a

433
00:16:17,958 --> 00:16:18,439
flare.

434
00:16:18,619 --> 00:16:20,459
Many have folding wings,

435
00:16:20,459 --> 00:16:22,683
so more jets fit on a crowded

436
00:16:22,683 --> 00:16:23,066
ship.

437
00:16:23,246 --> 00:16:25,971
A navy fighter is basically a land

438
00:16:25,971 --> 00:16:29,739
fighter rebuilt to survive the harshest airport

439
00:16:29,739 --> 00:16:30,781
in the world.

440
00:16:31,131 --> 00:16:32,512
So here are the ten.

441
00:16:32,692 --> 00:16:35,519
Little wings in front for an unstable

442
00:16:35,519 --> 00:16:37,963
jet that only computers can fly.

443
00:16:38,143 --> 00:16:40,871
Wing extensions that make tornadoes.

444
00:16:41,051 --> 00:16:43,587
Tails that lean away from radar,

445
00:16:43,587 --> 00:16:44,935
doors with teeth,

446
00:16:44,935 --> 00:16:46,520
and a golden canopy.

447
00:16:46,700 --> 00:16:49,525
A bump that replaces moving parts,

448
00:16:49,525 --> 00:16:52,101
nozzles that steer with thrust,

449
00:16:52,101 --> 00:16:54,760
a seat that leans back against g

450
00:16:54,760 --> 00:16:57,918
force, a striped handle that has saved

451
00:16:57,918 --> 00:16:59,497
thousands of lives,

452
00:16:59,497 --> 00:17:02,322
and a hook for the shortest runway

453
00:17:02,322 --> 00:17:03,070
on earth.

454
00:17:03,420 --> 00:17:07,387
Fighter jets look strange because every part

455
00:17:07,387 --> 00:17:09,641
is the answer to a fight,

456
00:17:09,641 --> 00:17:12,437
against physics, against radar,

457
00:17:12,437 --> 00:17:14,240
or against an enemy.

458
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:17,421
If you want more machines explained part

459
00:17:17,421 --> 00:17:20,272
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

460
00:17:20,452 --> 00:17:22,459
And tell us in the comments which

461
00:17:22,459 --> 00:17:24,466
machine you want us to take apart

